    "name":  "Bladder cancer",
    "module":  "oncology",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Bladder cancer is the most common malignancy of the urinary tract, with urothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma accounting for over 90% of cases. The hallmark presentation is painless frank haematuria, which must be assumed malignant until proven otherwise. Irritative bladder symptoms (frequency, urgency, dysuria) may also occur, particularly in carcinoma in situ (CIS). Risk factors include smoking (responsible for ~50% of cases), occupational exposure to aromatic amines (dyes, rubber), cyclophosphamide use, and chronic bladder inflammation. Upper tract urothelial carcinoma can occur concurrently and requires investigation.",
